    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Why this comes first</h2>
    <p style="font-size:16px;color:#39414B;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px">Federal court filings live in a system called PACER. <strong>People in custody cannot access it.</strong> Someone on the outside has to pull the documents — and most families don't know the system exists, let alone which of the dozens of docket entries actually matter.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;color:#39414B;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px">That gap causes real damage. Families spend months waiting on a motion that was never viable, or miss one that was — because nobody ever read the judgment, the plea agreement, or the sentencing transcript side by side.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;color:#39414B;line-height:1.8">A § 2255 is built out of the record. So is a compassionate release motion. So is a First Step Act time-credit claim. The file isn't paperwork — it's the raw material.</p>
  </div>
</section>

